3164	7tsUU1pOrms	From Problem To Solution - iOS Conf SG 2019	2019-01-20 03:04:13	Speaker: Soroush Khanlou , khanlou.com\n\nWhat it is, and what it isn’t. He’ll define a language that we can use to discuss abstraction, and we’ll step through the process of building one. How do we build an abstraction from code that isn’t abstract? What qualities are we looking for in our abstractions? How do we make good code out of bad?\n\nAbout the Speaker\n\nSoroush Khanlou is a New York-based iOS developer. He blogs about programming at khanlou.com, mostly about making view controllers really short. He wants to make writing apps easier with an open-source framework he made called Instant Cocoa. In his free time, he runs, bakes bread and pastries, and collects suitcases.\n\nEvent URL: http://iosconf.sg/\n\nProduced by Engineers.SG	https://i.ytimg.com/vi/7tsUU1pOrms/default.jpg	https://i.ytimg.com/vi/7tsUU1pOrms/mqdefault.jpg	https://i.ytimg.com/vi/7tsUU1pOrms/hqdefault.jpg	2019-01-20 06:31:09.348827	2025-01-09 04:01:28.976048	\N	t	1	769
2877	gpY90gsr9Yk	Seeing eye to eye with CI - JuniorDevSG	2018-09-16 11:58:45	Speaker: Sarine Soon, Software Consultant, Mavericks Consulting\n\nContinuous integration has been known as a best practice for many years now. What exactly is being “integrated”? What are the benefits of CI? Why is CI always clubbed together with CD and can they be separate? At what point in a project should CI be introduced? Is there a project that wouldn’t benefit from setting up CI? How does CI fit in and synergize with other Extreme Programming practices? Get the answers to these questions and find out what makes CI so awesome!\n\nEvent Page: https://www.meetup.com/Junior-Developers-Singapore/events/254063930/\n\nProduced by Engineers.SG\nRecorded by: Woo Huiren\n\nHelp us caption & translate this video!\n\nhttps://amara.org/v/lR1l/	https://i.ytimg.com/vi/gpY90gsr9Yk/default.jpg	https://i.ytimg.com/vi/gpY90gsr9Yk/mqdefault.jpg	https://i.ytimg.com/vi/gpY90gsr9Yk/hqdefault.jpg	2018-09-16 12:09:06.601404	2025-02-19 13:01:20.429899	\N	t	1	113
1890	fG9D6nwBj0A	Tiny JS Games on Microcontrollers - Istvan Szmozsanszky - talk.JS #49 July '17	2017-07-18 15:56:51	Speaker: Istvan Szmozsanszky (@flaki)\r\n\r\nSlides: http://talk.flak.is/play/sg/\r\n\r\nDescription:  Ever wanted to put some games on a microcontroller? No? Well, it's about time you unleashed your imagination! And in case you would be complaining about C++ right now... hold it right there, your trusty old friend, JavaScript will help you out here, too. ;)\r\n\r\nWe will have a look at MicroCanvas a tiny HTML5 Canvas games framework for creating pixelgraphic HTML5 games that work in any web browser, desktop or mobile - and for an added bonus see how these can be compiled and flashed on tiny microcontrollers to be played on small, arduino-based custom built gaming rigs.\r\n\r\nEvent Page: https://www.meetup.com/Singapore-JS/events/241600740/\r\n\r\nProduced by Engineers.SG\n\nHelp us caption & translate this video!\n\nhttp://amara.org/v/8iKa/	https://i.ytimg.com/vi/fG9D6nwBj0A/default.jpg	https://i.ytimg.com/vi/fG9D6nwBj0A/mqdefault.jpg	https://i.ytimg.com/vi/fG9D6nwBj0A/hqdefault.jpg	2017-07-18 16:32:36.779454	2024-07-20 12:01:22.118582	\N	t	1	177
